{
  "dom_id": "step-q80265",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1],$ and\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R\u2216D\\end{aligned}$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099625/assets/66f4c5d8824bf692d096ed533d90c9fdcf80c3725828e9033308757a0cdaa779.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and III equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function II. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\u2216D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,\u2216\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
